WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:03.800
What is the most dangerous thing you can imagine?

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:07.759
An average person might say an apex predator
or a violent killer.

00:00:07.759 --> 00:00:13.160
Meanwhile, a researcher working for the SCP
Foundation might cite one of the XK end of

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:18.130
the world scenarios like the release of the
Devourer of Worlds as the most dangerous thing

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:19.640
they can imagine.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:24.539
But if you were to pose that question to an
astrophysicist, ask what they think is the

00:00:24.539 --> 00:00:30.339
most dangerous thing they can imagine, then
you’re likely to get only one answer: a

00:00:30.339 --> 00:00:31.699
black hole.

00:00:31.699 --> 00:00:36.290
That area of space that pulls in anything
and everything around it with its immense

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:37.390
gravity.

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:42.430
Whether it’s planets or asteroids or even
light itself, nothing can escape the pull

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:44.230
of a black hole.

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:49.030
Around every black hole is an area known as
the event horizon, and once you go past this

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:52.050
boundary there’s no going back.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:56.400
If you were aboard a spacecraft that ended
up going past the event horizon of a black

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:00.780
hole, you’re essentially as doomed as anyone
can possibly be.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:06.049
According to certain laws of quantum mechanics,
black holes do have their own temperature,

00:01:06.049 --> 00:01:10.859
and will often emit what is known as Hawking
radiation, named after the renowned physicist

00:01:10.859 --> 00:01:12.119
Stephen Hawking.

00:01:12.119 --> 00:01:17.479
Given that black holes absorb everything,
they will also pull this radiation back into

00:01:17.479 --> 00:01:22.759
their own mass, shrinking as they gradually
collapse in on themselves.

00:01:22.759 --> 00:01:24.890
As for what’s inside a black hole?

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:26.299
Hard to say.

00:01:26.299 --> 00:01:30.549
Anything can be pulled into one, but nothing
can exist within it.

00:01:30.549 --> 00:01:34.470
Or at least, that’s what the world’s leading
scientists will tell you.

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:38.689
Other theories have been floating around out
there on where black holes might lead.

00:01:38.689 --> 00:01:44.450
Perhaps they might be doorways into other
universes and dimensions beyond our understanding.

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:49.799
Or maybe black holes are gaps in the fabric
of the space-time continuum, and falling through

00:01:49.799 --> 00:01:54.960
one might send you hurtling years into the
future, or even the past.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:00.880
And then, there’s SCP-123, something the
Foundation keeps well-guarded.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:08.319
SCP-123 consists of a grey geodesic sphere,
meaning it is a shape comprised of various

00:02:08.319 --> 00:02:11.830
connected triangles - kind of like the Epcot
Center.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:18.819
Built from an unknown material, the hollow
sphere housing SCP-123 consists of sixty triangles,

00:02:18.819 --> 00:02:22.790
with empty spaces between that allows someone
to view the center.

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:28.040
Within it is what looks like a much smaller
sphere, only around a single millimeter in

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:29.040
diameter.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:34.290
This inner sphere is completely black all
over, emitting zero light, and tests by Foundation

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:38.800
researchers have revealed that this dark sphere
will also not reflect light either.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:46.890
That’s because SCP-123 is, as you may have
guessed, a contained miniature black hole.

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:52.220
Much like the larger black holes found throughout
the solar system, the core of SCP-123 has

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:57.189
a strong gravitational force, capable of gently
pulling any objects that are within three

00:02:57.189 --> 00:03:00.500
meters of the outer geodesic sphere towards
it.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:05.019
Any object resting against the surface of
the outer sphere seems to become double its

00:03:05.019 --> 00:03:09.280
normal weight as it gets caught up in the
powerful forces within.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:13.549
However, while the pull of the black hole
is at least weakened somewhat by this outer

00:03:13.549 --> 00:03:19.280
layer, inside the casing the strength of the
black hole is dramatically increased.

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:24.000
You would be smart to leave your keys, wallet,
and phone in another room if you were approaching

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:29.579
SCP-123, as any small object that can fit
through the triangular gaps in the outer shell

00:03:29.579 --> 00:03:32.290
will be pulled into the black hole in the
center.

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:36.879
When brought close enough, solid objects will
rapidly accelerate as they are snatched up

00:03:36.879 --> 00:03:41.530
by the force of the anomalous sphere, only
for the object to vanish entirely from view

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:44.170
as it is lost to the void within.

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:48.870
According to tests conducted by Foundation
research teams, the same occurs if liquid

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:53.329
is ever poured through one of the outer sphere’s
gaps, and it will be sucked away into the

00:03:53.329 --> 00:03:55.939
black hole like water down a drain.

00:03:55.939 --> 00:04:02.049
Further observations made by the Foundation’s
researchers confirm that all light near SCP-123

00:04:02.049 --> 00:04:04.549
curves towards the black sphere in the center.

00:04:04.549 --> 00:04:09.569
Interestingly, black holes cannot normally
be seen or observed directly with the naked

00:04:09.569 --> 00:04:12.780
eye, due to them being able to pull in light.

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:17.750
So instead, astronomers and astrophysicists
will monitor changes in the area surrounding

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:21.310
a suspected black hole for any notable changes.

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:27.180
The miniature black hole of SCP-123 shares
these light bending properties, but also appears

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:31.569
to be at least partially observable while
contained within the outer geodesic shell.

00:04:31.569 --> 00:04:36.340
While the Foundation is still unsure as to
what the outer sphere is made of, it seems

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:40.830
to act as some sort of cage, keeping the black
hole stable and secure.

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:46.460
Otherwise, the cage itself, along with the
entire planet outside it, would presumably

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:48.530
be sucked into the center.

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:53.400
In fact, research staff have even been able
to roughly calculate the mass of the interior

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:58.810
black sphere, and have determined that the
cage surrounding it somehow reduces the gravitational

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:00.370
effects of the black hole.

00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:06.370
SCP Foundation experts state that the black
hole within SCP-123 currently has a mass of

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:12.210
approximately 10 to the 29th power kilograms
within the outer shell, but the cage’s dampening

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:17.180
effects may mean that the inner sphere’s
actual mass could be even higher.

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:22.669
Under ordinary circumstances, a black hole
with a 10 the 29th power kilograms of mass

00:05:22.669 --> 00:05:26.750
would have a Schwartzchild radius of about
two hundred meters.

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:31.870
Any professional astrophysicists in our audience
will already be well aware that a Schwartzchild

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:35.750
radius is the size of the black hole’s event
horizon.

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:40.199
If you remember earlier, we mentioned that
an event horizon in the area that surrounds

00:05:40.199 --> 00:05:45.509
a black hole, and going past this boundary
means that you now have a one way ticket into

00:05:45.509 --> 00:05:48.550
the black hole whether you like it or not.

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:54.310
The fact that SCP-123 doesn’t have such
a large event horizon, and that only objects

00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:58.660
placed within the outer sphere are pulled
in, shows that the only thing stopping that

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:04.940
black hole absorbing everything around it,
including all of us, is the cage it’s currently

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:06.259
held in.

00:06:06.259 --> 00:06:12.270
Nothing can escape the pull of a black hole,
no solid matter, no liquids, not even light.

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:17.310
However, in the case of SCP-123, it seems
that there is at least one state of matter

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:21.699
that this black hole is unable to absorb:
gases.

00:06:21.699 --> 00:06:27.039
Even though gaseous matter is affected by
the gravitational force of SCP-123, for some

00:06:27.039 --> 00:06:32.660
unknown reason gas cannot breach the gaps
in the outer shell surrounding the black hole.

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:37.460
The SCP Foundation is still studying why only
solids and liquids may pass through the cage

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:41.410
while gas cannot, but it’s almost certainly
for the best.

00:06:41.410 --> 00:06:47.210
After all, if SCP-123 could absorb gas and
pull other elements into its mass, there would

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:51.199
be little to stop it from sucking up all the
oxygen surrounding it.

00:06:51.199 --> 00:06:56.180
Somehow, either by an intentional design or
by the natural properties of the material

00:06:56.180 --> 00:07:00.790
it’s crafted from, the cage around the inner
sphere seems to protect the outside world

00:07:00.790 --> 00:07:02.830
from the miniature black hole.

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:07.020
It prevents the entire planet’s air as well
as the rest of the planet itself from being

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:13.180
pulled in and crushed, while also keeping
the black hole at a far more manageable size.

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:19.440
The two components of SCP-123 seem to behave
in a symbiotic manner, acting together as

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:20.440
one.

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:25.419
When someone moves the outer cage, the black
hole will follow and stay in a fixed position,

00:07:25.419 --> 00:07:28.720
hovering inside the center of the geodesic
casing.

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:34.039
Just what exactly makes both of SCP-123’s
parts behave in such a way has left even the

00:07:34.039 --> 00:07:36.400
Foundation’s top researchers baffled.

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:41.780
While a miniaturized black hole could potentially
cause devastating damage, for now at least,

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:48.550
SCP-123 doesn’t pose much of a threat as
long as it remains contained by the SCP Foundation.

00:07:48.550 --> 00:07:53.939
Within the confines of the geodesic cage,
the black hole itself is nearly harmless,

00:07:53.939 --> 00:07:56.550
only pulling in small objects.

00:07:56.550 --> 00:08:03.220
As a result, the Foundation has been able
to securely keep SCP-123 in one of their facilities.

00:08:03.220 --> 00:08:08.199
The contained miniature black hole is fastened
to a sturdy table with the use of straps and

00:08:08.199 --> 00:08:09.199
chains.

00:08:09.199 --> 00:08:13.560
However, personnel are forbidden from affixing
any hooks to the gaps in the outer casing,

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:17.910
otherwise these run the risk of being dragged
into the black hole’s gravitational pull,

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:21.280
potentially along with whatever the hooks
were attached to.

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:26.510
The Foundation’s researchers monitor the
black hole 24 hours a day, 7 days a week,

00:08:26.510 --> 00:08:28.069
52 weeks a year.

00:08:28.069 --> 00:08:33.690
Devices for measuring the gravitational force
of SCP-123 are placed within the same room

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:39.070
the black hole is kept, but at a safe distance
of one hundred meters away.

00:08:39.070 --> 00:08:44.750
Staff are also instructed to never place objects
within the outer shell of SCP-123, and are

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:49.350
prohibited from inserting any objects through
the gaps in the geodesic sphere unless an

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:51.790
experiment is taking place.

00:08:51.790 --> 00:08:58.890
At all times, SCP-123 is to be treated with
the utmost care, as if it was a fragile object.

00:08:58.890 --> 00:09:04.300
And for all the Foundation knows of the unidentified
outer sphere, it could well be.

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:08.440
If the cage containing the black hole was
ever breached, anything within two hundred

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:12.420
meters would be pulled into the center of
SCP-123.

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:16.839
As all forms of nearby matter were absorbed,
the black hole would begin to increase in

00:09:16.839 --> 00:09:22.180
size, pulling anything and everything into
itself as it continued to grow.

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:27.890
Before long, the entirety of Planet Earth
would be collapsing in on itself, all pulled

00:09:27.890 --> 00:09:31.300
towards one single point until the planet
fractured.

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:36.350
Perhaps the SCP-123 black hole would release
and then reabsorb enough Hawking radiation

00:09:36.350 --> 00:09:41.700
to seal itself, but by that point, the world
as we know it would be gone.

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:46.970
A true XK-Class End of the World Scenario,
with pretty much nothing the Foundation can

00:09:46.970 --> 00:09:49.750
do to stop it once it’s already started.

00:09:49.750 --> 00:09:55.130
Even SCP-2000 would be worthless once it was
sucked inside the miniature black hole.

00:09:55.130 --> 00:10:02.000
So, when we say that SCP-123 needs to be handled
with extreme care, we truly mean it.

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:06.950
The fate of the world depends on it, meaning
no member of the Foundation should ever shake

00:10:06.950 --> 00:10:10.480
the sphere around or exert any kind of force
on it.

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:16.561
During any transfers, SCP-123 is not to be
transported over large bodies of water, in

00:10:16.561 --> 00:10:20.529
case it begins to pull the liquid into its
center.

00:10:20.529 --> 00:10:24.300
Researchers working near and studying the
miniature black hole even have to adhere to

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:29.320
a strict dress code to avoid their clothes
getting absorbed into the spatial anomaly.

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:34.380
Any member of Foundation staff interacting
with SCP-123 is instructed that they must

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:40.779
wear tight-fitting clothing, so no straps
or laces, no chains or other dangling jewellery,

00:10:40.779 --> 00:10:42.940
and long hair has to be tied back.

00:10:42.940 --> 00:10:46.180
Sounds a lot like a school uniform dress code,
right?

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:50.170
It might not be all that fashionable, but
it’s a small price to pay to avoid getting

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:55.540
painfully pulled through the small triangular
gaps in the outer shell, into the black hole

00:10:55.540 --> 00:10:56.540
within.

00:10:56.540 --> 00:11:02.410
The only question left is, what do you do
with a tiny, perpetually-stable black hole?

00:11:02.410 --> 00:11:07.180
When left alone, SCP-123 doesn’t really
seem to have much of a purpose.

00:11:07.180 --> 00:11:11.850
But, it’s certainly good at getting rid
of things, so well that you’d never, ever

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:13.340
see them again.

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:18.150
The contained miniature black hole’s gravitational
force has led to some higher ranking individuals

00:11:18.150 --> 00:11:25.250
in the SCP Foundation discussing SCP-123’s
potentially usefulness as a disposal unit.

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:29.160
After all, nothing you’d throw into it would
ever come back.

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:33.680
However, the head researcher studying this
phenomena is concerned about the structural

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:37.329
integrity of the geodesic cage that the black
hole is kept in.

00:11:37.329 --> 00:11:42.930
Anyone looking to dispose of something using
SCP-123 must first get this doctor’s approval

00:11:42.930 --> 00:11:46.700
to do so, or request a formal hearing with
a Foundation higher-up.

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:52.590
Otherwise, any interactions with the miniature
black hole are restricted, with experiments

00:11:52.590 --> 00:11:58.490
still ongoing to determine how strong that
outer cage is, and just how long it’s going

00:11:58.490 --> 00:12:00.180
to hold for.

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:04.050
Because if it ever breaks, trust us, you’ll
know...

00:12:04.050 --> 00:12:10.480
Now go check out “SCP-3001 - Red Reality”
and “SCP-2317 - The Devourer of Worlds / A

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:14.759
Door to Another World” for more truly cosmic
SCPs!

